Every neighbourhood of V splits into two disjoint proper neighbourhoods of V whose union is the original set.

Write the neighbourhood as a nonempty level set at level k with mask m, and choose a residue ℓ₀ below 2^k whose bit is set. Upsampling the mask to level k + 1 sets both the bit ℓ₀ and the bit ℓ₀ + 2^k. Let one piece be the singleton-bit level set at ℓ₀, and let the other be the level set of the upsampled mask with that bit removed by exclusive or. Both are nonempty neighbourhoods of V. Their intersection is empty, because a point of the singleton-bit piece has residue ℓ₀, where the exclusive-or mask has that bit clear. Their union is the original level set, because the upsampled mask agrees bitwisely with the disjunction of the singleton bit and the exclusive or, and upsampling preserves the level set. Neither piece equals the original set: if the first did, the second would be contained in it and hence empty by disjointness, and symmetrically if the second did.
